  "analysis": "We need to compute the beauty (number of connected components of equal numbers) for many submatrices that span all rows (1..n) and columns l..r, where n <= 10, and m, q <= 1e5. A straightforward per-query flood-fill is too slow. A standard approach for small n is to build a segment tree over columns, where each node stores a compact summary of the submatrix for its column range. Each node keeps:\n- total components inside the segment (ans)\n- boundary information for the leftmost and rightmost columns: left_vals, right_vals (the actual values for each of the n rows at the segment boundaries)\n- boundary component labels for the left and right boundaries: Lids, Rids (size n), which label the boundary cells to a small set of boundary components touching the segment's left or right border\n- k: number of distinct boundary components (labels) touching either border (0..k-1)\nLeaves (single column) are straightforward: ans is the number of vertical runs in that column; Lids/Rids are the run IDs; left_vals/right_vals are the column values. Merging two adjacent nodes A (left) and B (right) involves connecting components across the boundary where A.right_vals[i] == B.left_vals[i] for each row i, by unioning A's boundary component containing the right boundary cell with B's boundary component containing the left boundary cell. The total number of components reduces by the number of successful unions. The new segment’s boundary labels are then recomputed by compressing the DSU roots that appear on the new left and right boundaries. Because n <= 10, the number of boundary components is small (at most about 2n), and each merge is cheap. A segment tree then answers each query in O(log m) time. The overall complexity is O((n * m) + q * log m) with small constants, which fits the constraints. The implementation uses fixed-size arrays for Node to keep memory reasonable (n <= 10).\nThe plan is to implement the described segment tree with a small DSU (disjoint-set union) per merge.",
  "plan": "Steps to implement:\n- Build a C++17 program implementing a segment tree over columns (size m).\n- Define a Node struct with: empty flag, ans, k, left_vals[10], right_vals[10], Lids[10], Rids[10]. Use fixed-size arrays (MAXN = 10) to avoid per-node vector overhead.\n- Implement leaf creation for a single column: compute vertical runs to get ans and assign Lids/Rids as run IDs; fill left_vals/right_vals with the column values; set k to number of runs.\n- Implement merge(A, B): if either is empty, return the other. Otherwise:\n  - ans = A.ans + B.ans\n  - DSU over kA + kB (kA = A.k, kB = B.k)\n  - For each row i, if A.right_vals[i] == B.left_vals[i], unite(A.Rids[i], kA + B.Lids[i]); decrement ans on successful unions.\n  - Build new boundary mapping: map DSU roots corresponding to A.Lids[i] and to B.Rids[i] (via A.Lids and B.Rids) to new compact IDs 0..k-1; set C.Lids[i] and C.Rids[i] accordingly; set C.k to the number of distinct roots used on the boundaries.\n  - Propagate left_vals = A.left_vals and right_vals = B.right_vals.\n- Implement a SegTree class with build, query. Query returns a Node for the requested [l, r] with merging as needed.\n- In main(), read n, m, q, read the n x m matrix, build the segment tree, then answer each query by querying the segment tree for [l, r] and printing node.ans.\n- Test with the provided sample to verify correctness.\n- Provide a complete solution as a single C++ file and instructions to compile and run with the sample input.",
